  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "The graph of a cubic $y=P(x)$ has exactly two $x$-intercepts, located at $x=1$ and $x=2$. The graph passes through the point $(0,8)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p28-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Is this enough information to determine $P(x)$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. Either $x=1$ or $x=2$ can be the double root.",
          "worked": "A cubic with exactly two distinct real roots must have one double root and one simple root. Therefore, either $P(x)=a(x-1)^2(x-2)$ or $P(x)=a(x-1)(x-2)^2$. The given information does not determine which root is doubled.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If not, then find all possible cubics satisfying these conditions.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=-4(x-1)^2(x-2)$ or $P(x)=-2(x-1)(x-2)^2$.\n\nAllow either intercept to be the double root, then use $P(0)=8$ to find the leading coefficient.",
          "worked": "If $x=1$ is the double root:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=a(x-1)^2(x-2)\\\\P(0)&=a(-1)^2(-2)\\\\8&=-2a\\\\a&=-4.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $P(x)=-4(x-1)^2(x-2)$.\n\nIf $x=2$ is the double root:\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=a(x-1)(x-2)^2\\\\P(0)&=a(-1)(-2)^2\\\\8&=-4a\\\\a&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $P(x)=-2(x-1)(x-2)^2$.\n\nThese are the only possibilities because the two root multiplicities must add to 3.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
